    "q-159330": {
      "dom_id": "step-q159330",
      "name": "Question 13",
      "problem": "The joint CDF of two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & x<0\\,\\text{or}\\,y<0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{4}{x}^{2}{y}^{3/2}, & 0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 1 \\\\ {F}_{X}(x), & 0\\le x\\le 2,\\,y>1 \\\\ {F}_{Y}(y), & x>2,\\,0\\le y\\le 1 \\\\ 1, & x>2,\\,y>1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${F}_{X}$ and ${F}_{Y}$ are the marginal CDFs of $X$ and $Y,$ respectively. Find the joint PDF $f(x,y)$ for $0\\le x\\le 2$ and $0\\le y\\le 1.$",
      "worked_solution": "Since the partial derivatives of $F(x,y)$ are defined, the joint probability density function $f(x,y)$ is determined by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\frac{{∂}^{2}}{∂x∂y}F(x,y).\n$$\n\nFor $0\\le x\\le 2$ and $0\\le y\\le 1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =\\frac{{∂}^{2}}{∂x∂y}F(x,y) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{2}{y}^{3/2})) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{3/2})) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}{y}^{1/2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{8}{y}^{1/2}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{8}{y}^{1/2}\\cdot 2x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}x{y}^{1/2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOtherwise, $f(x,y)=0.$\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{4}x{y}^{1/2}, & 0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 1 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
