    "q-159315": {
      "dom_id": "step-q159315",
      "name": "Question 15",
      "problem": "The joint CDF of two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-1)(y-1)}{xy}, & x\\ge 1,\\,y\\ge 1 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the joint PDF $f(x,y)$ for $x\\ge 1$ and $y\\ge 1.$",
      "worked_solution": "Since the partial derivatives of $F(x,y)$ are defined, the joint probability density function $f(x,y)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\frac{{∂}^{2}}{∂x∂y}F(x,y).\n$$\n\nFor $x\\ge 1$ and $y\\ge 1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =\\frac{{∂}^{2}}{∂x∂y}F(x,y) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{(x-1)(y-1)}{xy})) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{x-1}{x}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}(1-\\frac{1}{y})) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{x-1}{x}\\cdot \\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(1-\\frac{1}{x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOtherwise, $f(x,y)=0.$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}, & x\\ge 1,\\,y\\ge 1 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
